    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/6E871BCB0EFD76A7A4C5061C331B232C/jobs/585422": {
      "title": "Adjunct Lecturer, Global Public Health",
      "description": "Adjunct Lecturer, Global Public Health You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Adjunct Lecturer, Global Public Health Hybrid   •   Regis College Campus - Weston, MA 02493 Apply Overview Position Type Adjunct Apply Description Summary The Undergraduate Public Health Program invites applications for an Adjunct Faculty member to teach an undergraduate Global Public Health course in an in-person classroom setting. The successful candidate will bring current knowledge and practical experience in global health and will engage students in understanding major public health challenges, health disparities, and strategies for improving health outcomes across diverse populations and settings. Course: PBH 206 – Introduction to Global Health class to undergraduates who are majors or minors in public health or any other student with an interest in the topic. Class will be in-person and/or hybrid. The Global Public Health course introduces undergraduate students to the principles, practices, and challenges of public health from an international and cross-cultural perspective with a focus on low-income, low-resourced countries. Topics may include: Global patterns of health, disease, and mortality Social, economic, environmental, and political determinants of health Health disparities Communicable and non-communicable diseases Maternal, child, and reproductive health Global nutrition and food security Environmental and occupational health Public health systems and health policy Humanitarian emergencies and disaster response Global health organizations and partnerships Sustainable development and the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals Ethics, cultural competence, and community engagement in global health Case studies addressing contemporary global health challenge Essential Functions Teach an undergraduate Global Public Health course in person according to the approved course curriculum. Prepare and deliver engaging lectures, discussions, activities, and case-based learning experiences. Create an inclusive classroom environment that encourages critical thinking and respectful discussion. Integrate current global health research, data, policies, and real-world examples into course instruction. Develop and/or administer assignments, examinations, presentations, and other assessments. Provide timely, constructive feedback to students. Maintain accurate attendance and grading records. Be available to provide appropriate opportunities for student consultation. Communicate regularly with students regarding course expectations, assignments, and academic progress. Use the university's learning management system and other instructional technologies as required. Participate in department meetings, orientations, training, and other activities expected of adjunct faculty. Meet periodically with program director to ensure effective communication and clarity of expectations Comply with university policies and academic standards. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications and Skills Master's degree or higher in Public Health, Global Health, Epidemiology, Health Policy, International Development, or a closely related field. Professional or academic experience in global public health or a related discipline. Demonstrated ability to teach, present, or facilitate learning at the undergraduate level. Strong written and oral communication skills. Commitment to inclusive, culturally responsive, and student-centered teaching. Ability to incorporate current research, data, and real-world applications into teaching Organizational skills and ability to meet deadlines (grading, course management, reporting) Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree (DrPH, ScD, PhD, MD, or equivalent) in public health or a related field. College or university teaching experience. Experience working in international or cross-cultural public health settings. Professional experience with global health programs, NGOs, governmental or intergovernmental organizations, or community-based initiatives. Expertise in areas such as epidemiology, infectious disease, maternal and child health, health equity, environmental health, global health policy, or health systems. Experience using active-learning, case-based, experiential, or other evidence-informed teaching approaches. Experience with Learning Management Systems and course delivery Working Conditions/Physical Demands Normal in-person classroom environment, not subject to extremes in noise, odors, etc. May spend extended periods of time standing, sitting, or working on a computer Compensation : $4,607.61 per 3 credit course. Application Materials Interested individuals should submit all application materials through the Regis College application portal. Curriculum vitae or résumé Cover letter describing relevant global public health experience and teaching qualifications Contact information for [three] professional references The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to do this job. The above is not intended to be an exhaustive list of all responsibilities and duties required. External and internal applicants, as well as position incumbents who become disabled as defined under the Americans with Disabilities Act, must be able to perform the essential job functions (as listed) either unaided or with the assistance of a reasonable accommodation to be determined by management on a case-by-case basis. In employment, as in education, Regis College is committed to equal opportunity. Regis does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, age, religion, creed, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, ethnic or national origin, ancestry, citizenship, marital or parental status, physical or mental disability, genetic information, pregnancy, veteran’s status, membership in uniformed services or any other protected status. Regis College Mission Statement Share job details to About REGIS COLLEGE You Belong Here Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/6E871BCB0EFD76A7A4C5061C331B232C/jobs/585358": {
      "title": "Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs",
      "description": "Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs Regis College Campus - Weston, MA 02493 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description Leadership Profile Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs This leadership profile is intended to provide information about Regis College and the position of Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs. It is designed to assist qualified individuals in assessing their interest in this position. Opportunity and Position Summary The Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs (Provost) will be an energetic and innovative leader who will provide vision for all aspects of academic life at the university. The Provost will ensure both the high quality of academic programs and their alignment with student demand and workforce need; strengthen teaching and research; and support the success of diverse students in their academic work and post-graduation planning. The Provost will play a significant role on the university’s leadership team in achieving the institution's strategic goals, including expanding Regis' standing, locally and nationally, as a leader in the education of health professionals, building on the university’s reputation in nursing to encompass its broader scope of health professions programs, grounded in Regis' mission and in the durable capacities of the liberal arts, which underpin every major. The new Provost must be a seasoned, strategic leader with demonstrated accomplishments; the ability to embody and promote the university’s mission; and a clear sense of the complex challenges of an institution serving traditional and non-traditional undergraduate and graduate students in professional and in liberal arts schools, with both campus based and online offerings. This is a role that depends on partnership. The Provost will work closely with colleagues across every division – enrollment management, advancement, finance, student affairs, ministry and service, and marketing and communications – and success will come through building shared purpose rather than directing from the academic side alone. Extraordinary communication skills are essential, as is the ability to foster a culture that is both collaborative and innovative. The successful candidate will be both empathetic and accountable. Regis is a community that knows its people by name and takes seriously the CSJ commitment to excellence tempered by gentleness. It is also an institution that must make disciplined decisions about programs, resources, and structures. The next Provost will need to do both: to lead change with genuine care for the people affected by it, and to hold themselves and others to clear expectations and measurable results. The ideal candidate will bring experience in online education, a demonstrated record of improving outcomes for students with diverse needs and experiences, and success in developing partnerships that benefit both Regis and its partners. In addition to experience comparable to that of a provost, vice president, or dean, the Provost will have an earned doctorate or terminal professional degree and a record of scholarly achievement that would merit a faculty appointment in one of the university’s schools. As chief academic officer, the Provost leads the deans of the School of Arts, Sciences and Education, the School of Health Sciences, the School of Business and Communication, and the Richard and Sheila Young School of Nursing, and holds responsibility for all academic programs, the Library, the Registrar, academic advising, the Office of Strategic Partnerships, and the Office of Career and Professional Development. Regis College was founded in 1927 by the Congregation of the Sisters of St. Joseph of Boston (CSJ). The Sisters founded Regis College at a time when higher education options for women were limited. The mission of providing access to an education based on CSJ values and striving for excellence tempered with gentleness endures today on a co-educational campus that prides itself on welcoming all without distinction. The campus is located in Weston, Massachusetts, in the greater Boston area. Inquiries, nominations and applications are invited. Interested individuals should provide a letter describing their interest in and qualification for the position, a curriculum vitae and the names and contact information of three references. This search will be conducted maintaining full confidentiality of all candidates; references will not be contacted without the prior knowledge and approval of the candidate. The Role of the Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs The essential duties and responsibilities for the Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs are as follows: Lead and supervise the deans and academic leadership team, setting clear expectations, developing talent, and holding the academic enterprise accountable for results. The academic leadership team includes: Assistant Provost, Deans, Registrar, Director of Strategic Partnerships, Director of Career and Professional Development, Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with Regis College’s policies and applicable laws. In partnership with Enrollment Management, Finance, and Marketing and Communications, lead systematic academic portfolio optimization – evaluating programs against student demand, workforce need, academic quality, and financial contribution, and acting on what the evidence shows. Develop and expand the academic portfolio beyond the university’s current reputation, building strength across various helping professions while sustaining excellence in nursing and ensuring that liberal arts formation remains foundational across every program, and that its value is visible to students, families, and employers. Build and steward strategic partnerships with employers, health systems, community colleges, and community organizations that expand learning, career, and enrollment opportunities. Advance teaching and learning practices suited to today's students and technologies – using artificial intelligence where it genuinely deepens learning, setting it aside where it does not, and protecting the relationship between teacher and student that no technology can replace. Ensure that artificial intelligence is treated as curriculum and not only as pedagogy, so that Regis graduates enter their professions able to use these tools capably, critically, and ethically if required. Oversee the integration of academic advising and career development to strengthen retention, persistence, and post-graduation outcomes. Serve as senior staff liaison to the Board Committee on Academic Affairs and supports the work of the Board. Recommend to the President all faculty to appointed to rank, promotion, tenure, sabbatical leave, salary, and compensation. Perform other related duties as required. Opportunities and Expectations for Leadership Advance the Strategic Plan The university is in the development phase of Regis's 2026-2030 strategic plan. The planning framework consists of five strategic pillars and sixteen supporting goals. The initiative proposal process is currently underway. A link to a graphic representation of the plan is available here. The provost will work closely with the president's cabinet, steering committee, deans, faculty, and staff to lead initiatives in several key areas, including retention, new enrollment, campus vibrancy, and operational excellence. Set and Execute Academic Portfolio Strategy Regis offers a distinctive range of programs and a complete academic ladder, from associate through doctoral study, delivered on campus and online. The Provost will bring strategic and data-informed discipline to that portfolio: evaluating programs against demonstrated demand, workforce need, quality, and financial contribution; investing in what is working and has room to grow; consolidating or sunsetting what no longer serves students or the university; and identifying the adjacent opportunities that Regis is uniquely positioned to pursue. The university intends to expand what it is known for. Regis holds a national reputation in nursing and has real but less visible strength across health sciences, public health, and health-adjacent programs in social work, counseling, and education. The Provost will build that visibility through new and reshaped programs, through strategic partnerships that connect students to employers, and through robust student outcomes. The Provost will also ensure that the liberal arts remain the foundation of a Regis education rather than a competing interest. Every professional program at Regis rests on the formation the liberal arts provide, and the Provost will be expected to make that case persuasively to faculty, prospective families, and employers. Lead and Develop the Faculty The Provost will recognize the need for a solid infrastructure to support a growing number of adjunct faculty working across the country in support of the online initiatives. A focus on faculty diversity, celebration of faculty accomplishments as well as support of professional development will be key to that success. The Provost will work with the deans and faculty to build a culture that is both supportive and accountable – clarifying policy and practice, establishing consistent and equitable standards for workload, compensation, stipends, and course release across schools, and investing in professional development that helps faculty teach well in a rapidly changing environment. The Provost will also bring faculty closer to what happens after graduation, so that career outcomes become a shared concern of the curriculum rather than a service students seek out on their own. Central to this work is the vitality of the residential campus. Regis students choose a small university because of what happens between classes – the office-hours conversation, the faculty member who notices a student is struggling, the mentorship that turns into a recommendation letter or a career. That kind of formation depends on presence. The Provost will lead a renewed commitment to an active, visible faculty presence on campus for students enrolled in campus-based programs, working with the deans to ensure that faculty availability, advising, and engagement in the life of the university are consistent expectations rather than individual choices. The Provost should be someone who believes in the value of a residential academic community, can articulate that value persuasively, and is prepared to build the shared expectations that sustain it. Advance Student Success and Career Readiness Regis measures itself by what happens to its students – whether they persist, whether they graduate, and where they land. The Provost will own the academic contribution to retention and completion and will lead the integration of academic advising with career and professional development so that students experience one coherent path rather than a set of separate offices. The Provost will use data to identify where students struggle and to act on it, will strengthen experiential learning and clinical placement across programs, and will work with employer partners to ensure that what Regis teaches is what graduates need. The goal is graduates who are prepared for the professions: technically excellent as well as adaptable, compassionate, ethical, critical thinkers and clear communicators. Qualifications Professional Qualiﬁcations and Personal Qualities Regis College seeks a forward thinking, entrepreneurial and proven academic leader for the position of Provost and Vice President of Academic Affairs. The successful candidate will embrace and uphold the university’s mission and values and will possess: the ability to engage the faculty around a strategic vision for the future of academic programs; the communication and interpersonal skills necessary to advocate for that future; an understanding and regard for the work of faculty both in the classroom and as members of their scholarly and professional communities; and an overriding focus on student success, diversity and inclusion. They must possess the ability to listen to a broad array of constituents and the fortitude to make tough decisions that put the best interests of the university first. Candidate will also demonstrate: Leadership and vision: Significant, relevant and progressively responsible experience in academic administration, preferably at an institution similar to Regis; ability to serve as a strong, self-assured leader and scholar with the intellectual stature to inspire the faculty, staff and students with a sense of pride, purpose and quality; a commitment to the mission of the university and its strategic priorities; Advocate for Regis College Mission: The capacity to promote the Regis mission through a deep understanding of and commitment to the ideals and goals of Catholic education and the intellectual tradition that shaped them; Strategic planning expertise: The capability to both develop and implement academic and university-wide strategic plans; the ability to imagine new possibilities for the university and to develop relevant and realistic goals and initiatives for the university; Leadership for faculty: The ability to work effectively with faculty and a successful track record recruiting, developing, supporting and retaining an outstanding, diverse faculty; Student success: Demonstrated success in cultivating a student-centered environment including administrative processes, especially in the areas of academic support services, and career and professional development, to ensure student success; Management skills: Demonstrated success in leading and managing faculty and staff at an institution with a varied academic portfolio; a collegial, collaborative management style and the ability to be a decisive leader; strong negotiation, management, organizational and supervisory skills; data-informed decision-making; Financial acumen: Strong financial and budgetary competency; the ability to strategically allocate resources and navigate the pressure of competing demands on finite resources; Commitment to diversity and inclusion: A strong demonstrated commitment to diversity and inclusion among faculty, staff and students, including in scholarship and curricula; Interpersonal skills: A leadership style that reflects a commitment to transparency and authenticity, the ability to listen carefully and openness to listening to the concerns of all members of the university community; the political insight to work effectively in a complex environment; and ability to cultivate a high-trust culture throughout the Regis community; Commitment to shared governance: A strong commitment to shared governance structures and principles; experience supporting and constructing avenues for effective faculty governance and participation as well as facilitating open and authentic communication between faculty and administration; Entrepreneurial spirit: An entrepreneurial spirit and a willingness to take considered risks and seize opportunities; and Personal qualities: Intellectual curiosity, sophistication, empathy, creativity, and high standards for personal and professional achievement; grace under pressure and a sense of humor; the character and maturity to serve as an engaged citizen; and a passion for improving the human condition. Education and Experience The successful candidate will have: a record of teaching, scholarship and service that merits appointment in one of the faculties of the university; an earned doctorate or other appropriate terminal degree from an accredited institution; a distinguished record as a teacher, scholar and academic administrator in positions of increasing responsibility; and a minimum of eight years of progressive leadership experience in higher education or the equivalent combination of education and experience; a record as an academic administrator who led significant programmatic growth; and, experience in successfully leading change with courage and compassion About Regis College Regis College was founded by the Sisters of St. Joseph of Boston in 1927 as a residential, Catholic women’s liberal arts institution, at a time when higher education options for women were limited. Its mission was to strive for excellence and welcome all women without distinction. Over its history, grounded in its mission, it has evolved to meet the changing needs of the day. During the 1970s, Regis began granting its first graduate degrees. In 2007, it became a co-ed institution and launched its first doctoral program, the Doctor of Nursing Practice. This was followed by a second doctoral program, the Doctor of Education in Higher Education Leadership which was launched in 2013. In 2017, Regis embarked on a new strategic initiative, offering its first fully online programs. Today, Regis offers programs in the arts and sciences, education, business and communication, professional studies, and nationally recognized programs in nursing and health sciences. With many programs offered fully online, the university’s reach is national. The mission of the university remains consistent with its founding values. Rooted in the Catholic intellectual tradition and inspired by the social justice values of its founders, Regis is a welcoming and inclusive community. The university provides access to an academically rigorous education grounded in the liberal arts tradition through innovative learning opportunities and collaborative engagement at all degree levels. Regis lives up to its motto, Via Veritas Vita (“the way and the truth and the life”), which reflects its tradition of empowering students to seek knowledge to pursue meaningful life experiences that contribute to our global community. The university offers academic programs within four schools—the School of Arts, Sciences and Education, the Marshall M. Sloane School of Business and Communication, the Richard and Sheila Young School of Nursing, and the School of Health Sciences—as well as bachelor’s degree completion and certificates through the Theresa Wood Lavine Professional Studies programs. The university is also home to four well-regarded centers including the Children’s Center, Autism Center, Fine Arts Center, and Center for Ministry and Service. Regis integrates hands-on, experiential learning across the curriculum. All students gain direct experience through clinical rotations, fieldwork, and internships, assuring each student gains the skills they need for their chosen career. Opportunities for global experience also help shape students into leaders, ready to have a significant impact on their chosen profession. The faculty, accomplished in their respective disciplines and fields, are dedicated to offering personalized support, assuring students achieve their academic and professional goals. Leadership Regis is governed by a Board of Trustees and is a sponsored ministry of the Sisters of St. Joseph of Boston. Faculty, staff, and students are represented by the President’s Cabinet, Faculty Council, Graduate Student Advisory Council, and Student Government Association. Robin Lynn Cautin , PhD, became the 11th president of Regis College on July 1, 2026. She brings more than two decades of experience in higher education, shaped by roles as a faculty member, associate provost, dean, and chief academic officer. Throughout her career, she has focused on aligning financial resources and organizational structures with strategic priorities, strengthening student outcomes while ensuring that decisions are guided by both mission and long-term financial sustainability. As she begins her presidency, Dr. Cautin is focused on positioning Regis for continued strength and stability; building on its mission to educate the whole person and serve the dear neighbor without distinction and ensuring th"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/6E871BCB0EFD76A7A4C5061C331B232C/jobs/585500": {
      "title": "Director of Finance, Academic Affairs",
      "description": "Director of Finance, Academic Affairs You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Director of Finance, Academic Affairs Hybrid   •   Regis College Campus - Weston, MA 02493 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description SUMMARY The Director of Finance for Academic Affairs is responsible for maintaining operating budgets for Academic Affairs to ensure complete and accurate financial data is readily available. This position is responsible for preparing and maintaining compliance with internal and external policies and procedures; developing revenue budgets in close collaboration with Admissions Offices, Deans, and Program Directors; monitoring the financial results and maintaining effective internal controls and policies related to our OPM; preparing, evaluating, and performing ongoing program assessments in a timely and accurate manner; and making recommendations to leadership to maximize efficiency and effectiveness. ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS Report directly to the Provost; responsible for working with Deans, the Associate Provost and all other Academic unit leaders to provide budget development, preparation, analysis and management for an Academic Affairs division operating budget of approximately $41M. Responsible for responding to and preparing ad hoc financial analysis necessary to respond to internal and external financial information requests, including program assessments as deemed appropriate by Provost. Work and interact closely with the Provost, Deans, Associate Provost, Assistant Provost, all Academic division administrators, the Business Office, Human Resources, Vice Presidents and all personnel deemed appropriate for monitoring budgets and operational needs, while maintaining a high degree of confidentiality. Responsible for Graduate revenue budget development; facilitate Graduate budget workshop sessions, monitoring enrollment numbers and revenue forecasting by working directly the leadership in Enrollment Management, MarCom, Deans, and Program Directors. Work closely with Deans and Program Directors on the development of any new undergraduate or graduate programs to ensure margin optimization. Monitor the financial status of all academic programs and provide Provost with recommendations on continuance of and/or investment in academic programs. Monitor and analyze all budgets to ensure replacement, new or incremental hires are within the budgetary constraints of the School and the Academic Affairs division. This requires intricate understanding of the full budget and how to reallocate resources from within to best optimize efficiencies. Prepare necessary budget transfers and journal entries to record transactional data in the general ledger. Analyze results monthly, quarterly, and at year end during close process. Ensure accurate and timely reporting and respond to inquiries within and outside the organization in an effective manner. Analyze budget vs. actual expenditure and generate reports and statistical data, including detailed analysis and modeling to facilitate university results based on resources. Continue to evaluate current fiscal processes and procedures while making recommendations to improve, implement and test as deemed necessary. Responsible for review and approval of internal and external financial reporting documents for Academic Affairs division; maintain complete, accurate, and organized records and support for all transactions. Monitor financial results related to the OPM, Boundless Learning. Ensure all pertinent personnel have a full understanding of the key financial data necessary to maintain positive results; ensure timely follow-up and assist outreach to any personnel, internal and external, to ensure compliance with the contract. Responsible for all Academic Affairs new or replacement staff requisition approvals while ensuring hiring is within budgetary constraints. Evaluate and implement internal controls on a regular basis and work with all units to ensure collaborative efforts. OTHER DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Perform other related duties as required. Qualifications SKILLS/EXPERIENCE/TRAINING REQUIRED Bachelor’s degree in accounting or finance, CPA or related credentials with experience in progressively responsible positions; Excellent communication and interpersonal skills; Excellent analytical, problem-solving and organizational skills; Advanced working knowledge in Excel; knowledge of accounting software programming desired; Able to work effectively with all constituents and levels of management; Strong sense of teamwork with an ability to learn new responsibilities and work independently and as a team leader. WORKING CONDITIONS/PHYSICAL DEMANDS Normal office environment, not subject to extreme variations of temperature, noise, odors, etc. Regularly uses computer keyboards requiring eye-hand coordination and finger dexterity. COMPENSATION: $102,000 - $112,000 annually. The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to do this job. The above is not intended to be an exhaustive list of all responsibilities and duties required. External and internal applicants, as well as position incumbents who become disabled as defined under the Americans with Disabilities Act, must be able to perform the essential job functions (as listed) either unaided or with the assistance of a reasonable accommodation to be determined by management on a case-by-case basis. In employment, as in education, Regis College is committed to equal opportunity. Regis does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, age, religion, creed, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, ethnic or national origin, ancestry, citizenship, marital or parental status, physical or mental disability, genetic information, pregnancy, veteran’s status, membership in uniformed services or any other protected status. Regis College Mission Statement Share job details to About REGIS COLLEGE You Belong Here Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/6E871BCB0EFD76A7A4C5061C331B232C/jobs/582777": {
      "title": "Adjunct Lecturer, Sociology",
      "description": "Adjunct Lecturer, Sociology You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Adjunct Lecturer, Sociology Regis College Campus - Weston, MA 02493 Apply Overview Position Type Adjunct Apply Description Summary The Department of Humanities at Regis College invites applications for an Adjunct Lecturer to teach SO-201: Introduction to Sociology during the Spring 2027 semester. This course will be conducted in-person on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays from 10:00-10:50. Course Description This course introduces students to the major theories, theorists, concepts, and methods used in the study of society. It considers a variety of topics including culture, socialization, gender, racial, economic, and social class divisions. (Instructor has the freedom to construct a syllabus and decide on course content in consultation with department Chair, so long as course description and outcomes are unchanged.) Essential Functions Teaching SO-201 Ensuring appropriate pedagogical design, implementation, and assessment of the course Providing timely feedback to students and hold office hours at least 2 hours/week (this can be done via Zoom) Please Note: Adjunct instructors are NOT expected to attend school or department meetings (though they are welcome to), conduct academic advising, or serve on standing committees. Other Responsibilities Ability to interact in a positive manner with a diverse group of co-workers and students while upholding the College’s commitment to inclusive excellence. Carry out functions as they may be appropriate for the position. Qualifications Requirements An advanced degree (PhD preferred but not required) in a discipline related to the humanities and/or sociology At least two years teaching experience At least two references Working Conditions/Physical Demands Normal classroom environment, not subject to extremes in noise, odors, etc Compensation Master’s degree–prepared candidate: $3,485 per course Doctoral degree–prepared candidate: $3,700 per course The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to do this job. The above is not intended to be an exhaustive list of all responsibilities and duties required. External and internal applicants, as well as position incumbents who become disabled as defined under the Americans with Disabilities Act, must be able to perform the essential job functions (as listed) either unaided or with the assistance of a reasonable accommodation to be determined by management on a case-by-case basis. In employment, as in education, Regis College is committed to equal opportunity. Regis does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, age, religion, creed, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, ethnic or national origin, ancestry, citizenship, marital or parental status, physical or mental disability, genetic information, pregnancy, veteran’s status, membership in uniformed services or any other protected status. Please take a moment to review our Mission Statement and Strategic Plan . Share job details to About REGIS COLLEGE You Belong Here Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
